<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michael the Brave</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1558">1558</a>–<a href="/years/1601">1601</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Mihai Viteazul</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Michael the Brave (Mihai Viteazul) was prince of Wallachia from 1593 to 1601. He rebelled against Ottoman overlordship, winning at Călugăreni in 1595, and in 1599 and 1600 took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> and Moldavia, briefly uniting the three principalities under one ruler. His rule soon collapsed and he was assassinated in 1601, but he later became a symbol of Romanian unity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michael-the-brave.jpg" alt="Michael the Brave" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aegidius Sadeler · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MihaiViteazul.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Michael was born in 1558 as Mihai Pătrașcu. He claimed to be the illegitimate son of the Wallachian prince Pătrașcu cel Bun, though some historians think he invented this descent and that his real father was most likely a Greek merchant. His mother was Theodora Kantakouzene. He rose quickly, becoming Ban of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/craiova">Craiova</a> in 1593, but a conflict with Prince Alexandru cel Rău forced him to flee that year. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> he secured Ottoman support, helped by a loan of 200,000 florins, and Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-iii">Murad III</a> invested him as prince in September 1593.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Michael soon turned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. In 1594 he joined the Christian alliance formed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-viii">Pope Clement VIII</a> and took several citadels near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. In August 1595 an Ottoman army under Sinan Pasha crossed the Danube. Outnumbered, Michael gave battle on marshy ground near Călugăreni and defeated it, but had too few troops to follow up and withdrew. The war went on until he made peace on 26 June 1599, lacking support from his allies. His rule rested on the Oltenian lords, and he passed a law binding serfs to aristocrats' land.</p><p>When Andrew Báthory demanded that Michael give up his throne, Michael marched into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> and defeated him at Șelimbăr on 18 October 1599, then entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alba-iulia">Gyulafehérvár</a> as imperial governor under Emperor Rudolf II. In April 1600 his troops entered Moldavia and reached Iași on 6 May, and he had a seal showing the three principalities. The Transylvanian Hungarian nobility rose against him with the help of General Giorgio Basta, while Polish forces drove his troops from Moldavia and Wallachia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Michael visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> in 1601 and received 100,000 florins from Rudolf II. Allied with Basta, he defeated the Hungarian army at Guruslău. A few days later, on 9 August 1601, Basta, who wished to control <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> himself, had him killed near Câmpia Turzii, on the emperor's order. From the middle of the nineteenth century he was seen as a unifier and precursor of a modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, though the historian A. D. Xenopol held that there was no national element in his politics.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Michael the Brave” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ottoman control tightened after 1538, and the chronic contest among boyar factions and claimants, each seeking support in Istanbul, Poland or Transylvania, wore down the principality’s autonomy; <b>Michael the Brave of Wallachia</b> held it briefly in 1600.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Society rested on the boyars, the landowning class whose coalitions made and unmade princes, and on a peasantry whose lot worsened under Ottoman economic hegemony: serfdom was established under <b>Michael the Brave</b> to increase manorial revenues and effectively abolished by Constantine Mavrocordatos in 1746.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-wallachia">Principality of Wallachia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Târgu de Floci, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. Died in Turda, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.69,27.83&amp;year=1585" aria-label="Târgu de Floci on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5214 584 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Michael the Brave"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5664 854h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5664 854h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.69,27.83&amp;year=1585">What was here in 1585? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
